Review Practice Answers.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"I. MCQ 1. B  2. C  3. A  4. B  5. B  6. B  7. A  8. B  9. C  10. C 11. B  12. B  13. B  14. B  15. B   16. A  17. C  18. C  19. B  20. B  II. FRQ 1. (a) Prokaryotic DNA: Single circular chromosome in cytoplasm; no telomeres. Eukaryotic DNA: Multiple linear chromosomes in nucleus; telomeres protect ends from shortening. (b) Okazaki fragments: DNA polymerase synthesizes only 5’→3’, but lagging strand runs from 3’ to 5’. Lagging strand is replicated away from the replication fork → must be synthesized as short Okazaki fragments. Enzyme: DNA polymerase I replaces primers with DNA sequence; DNA ligase joins fragments (c) Increased mutations due to uncorrected errors, leading to diseases like cancer  2. (a) mRNA: 5′-AUG CAU GAA CGU-3′ Protein: Methionine (AUG) - Histidine (CAU) - Glutamic Acid (GAA) - Arginine (CGU)  (b) Mutation type: Substitution. Amino acid change: Original: Arginine (CGU) → Mutated: Proline (CCA). (c) 5' cap/3' poly-A tail: Protect mRNA from degradation; enable ribosome binding. Splicing: Removes introns (non-coding) → joins exons for the production of more functional protein from one DNA sequence.  3. (a) Cancer cells: Telomerase highly active → prevents telomere shortening → unlimited division  Somatic cells: Telomerase inactive → telomeres shorten → limits cell division (except for stem cells or fast-growing cells) (b) Plants: Beneficial → larger/stronger Animals: Lethal","MCQ,B,C,B,B,B,B,C,C,B,B,B,B,B,C,C,B,B,II,FRQ,Prokaryotic,DNA,Single,circular,chromosome,cytoplasm,telomeres,Eukaryotic,DNA,Multiple,linear,chromosomes,nucleus,telomeres,protect,ends,shortening,b,Okazaki,fragments,DNA,polymerase,synthesizes,lagging,strand,runs,Lagging,strand,replicated,away,replication,fork,must,synthesized,short,Okazaki,fragments,Enzyme,DNA,polymerase,replaces,primers,DNA,sequence,DNA,ligase,joins,fragments,c,Increased,mutations,due,uncorrected,errors,leading,diseases,like,cancer,mRNA,AUG,CAU,GAA,CGU,Protein,Methio