Review Practice.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"D) Crossing over  8. Alternative RNA splicing enables: A) Faster translation B) The production of multiple proteins from one gene C) Intron retention D) DNA replication  9. tRNA with anticodon AUU pairs with mRNA codon: A) ATG B) UAA C) none D) UAC  10. Sickle cell anemia's Glu→Val mutation is a: A) Silent mutation B) Nonsense mutation C) Missense mutation D) Frameshift mutation  11. Prokaryotic transcription/translation overlap due to: A) Circular DNA B) The lack of nucleus C) Stable mRNA D) Small ribosomes  12. Translocation causes CML(Chronic myelogenous leukemia) by: A) Deleting genes B) Activating uncontrolled cell division gene C) Inactivating repair D) Duplicating chromosomes  13. During DNA replication, DNA polymerase III without proofreading causes: A) Faster replication B) More mutations C) Telomere loss D) Primer failure  14. Polyploidy benefits plants by making them: A) Disease-resistant B) Larger/stronger C) Tastier D) Insect-resistant","Crossing,Alternative,RNA,splicing,enables,Faster,translation,B,production,multiple,proteins,one,gene,C,Intron,retention,DNA,replication,tRNA,anticodon,AUU,pairs,mRNA,codon,ATG,B,UAA,C,none,UAC,Sickle,cell,anemia,Glu,Val,mutation,Silent,mutation,B,Nonsense,mutation,C,Missense,mutation,Frameshift,mutation,Prokaryotic,transcription,translation,overlap,due,Circular,DNA,B,lack,nucleus,C,Stable,mRNA,Small,ribosomes,Translocation,causes,CML,Chronic,myelogenous,leukemia,Deleting,genes,B,Activating,uncontrolled,cell,division,gene,C,Inactivating,repair,Duplicating,chromosomes,DNA,replication,DNA,polymerase,III,without,proofreading,causes,Faster,replication,B,mutations,C,Telomere,loss,Primer,failure,Polyploidy,benefits,plants,making,Disease,resistant,B,Larger,stronger,C,Tastier,Insect,resistant"
Review Practice.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"15. Retroviruses violate central dogma by: A) DNA → protein B) RNA → DNA C) Protein → RNA D) RNA → protein  16. Eukaryotic RNA polymerase binds promoters via: A) Transcription factors B) tRNA C) Ribosomes D) Primers  17. A nonsense mutation creates: A) Same amino acid B) New amino acid C) Stop codon → nonfunctional protein D) Frameshift  18. Okazaki fragments joined by: A) Helicase B) DNA pol I C) DNA ligase D) RNA Primase  19. In the Hershey-Chase experiment, bacteriophages were grown with radioactive phosphorus-32 (³²P) to label their DNA. After allowing these phages to infect bacteria, where was the ³²P radioactivity primarily detected? A) In the empty protein coats outside the bacteria B) Inside the bacterial cells (host cytoplasm) C) In newly assembled phage particles D) In the liquid culture medium  20. RNA splicing keeps all of the following EXCEPT: A) Exons B) Introns C) Poly-A tails D) 5' caps","Retroviruses,violate,central,dogma,DNA,protein,B,RNA,DNA,C,Protein,RNA,RNA,protein,Eukaryotic,RNA,polymerase,binds,promoters,via,Transcription,factors,B,tRNA,C,Ribosomes,Primers,nonsense,mutation,creates,amino,acid,B,New,amino,acid,C,Stop,codon,nonfunctional,protein,Frameshift,Okazaki,fragments,joined,Helicase,B,DNA,pol,C,DNA,ligase,RNA,Primase,Hershey,Chase,experiment,bacteriophages,grown,radioactive,phosphorus,P,label,DNA,allowing,phages,infect,bacteria,P,radioactivity,primarily,detected,empty,protein,coats,outside,bacteria,B,Inside,bacterial,cells,host,cytoplasm,C,newly,assembled,phage,particles,liquid,culture,medium,RNA,splicing,keeps,following,EXCEPT,Exons,B,Introns,C,Poly,tails,caps"
