S Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Chapter 16 SoluBons 16.1 ProperBes of SoluBons 1. SoluBon FormaBon 3 Factors That Aﬀect How Fast a Substance Dissolves AgitaCon (SCrring): Dissolving occurs at the surface of solute parBcles. SBrring brings solvent (e.g., water) in contact with the solute's surface, speeding up the dissolving process. Note: AgitaBon does not inﬂuence the amount of solute that will dissolve. Temperature: Higher temperatures increase the kineBc energy of water molecules, causing them to move faster. This increases the frequency of collisions between solvent molecules and solute parBcles. ParCcle Size of the Solute: Smaller parBcles (e.g., granulated sugar) have a greater surface area exposed to the solvent, increasing the rate of dissolving. 2. Solubility -Saturated SoluBon: Contains the maximum amount of solute for a given quanBty of solvent at a constant temperature and pressure. -Dynamic equilibrium exists between the dissolved solute and any undissolved solute. Solubility is expressed as grams of solute per 100 g of solvent (g/100 g H₂O). -Unsaturated SoluBon: Contains less solute than a saturated soluBon. AddiBonal solute can dissolve unBl saturaBon is reached. Miscible Liquids: Liquids that dissolve in each other in all proporBons (e.g.,","Chapter,SoluBons,ProperBes,SoluBons,SoluBon,FormaBon,Factors,ect,Fast,Substance,Dissolves,AgitaCon,SCrring,Dissolving,occurs,surface,solute,parBcles,SBrring,brings,solvent,e,g,water,contact,solute,surface,speeding,dissolving,process,Note,AgitaBon,uence,amount,solute,dissolve,Temperature,Higher,temperatures,increase,kineBc,energy,water,molecules,causing,move,faster,increases,frequency,collisions,solvent,molecules,solute,parBcles,ParCcle,Size,Solute,Smaller,parBcles,e,g,granulated,sugar,greater,surface,area,exposed,solvent,increasing,rate,dissolving,Solubility,Saturated,SoluBon,Contains,maximum,amount,solute,given,quanBty,solvent,constant,temperature,pressure,Dynamic,equilibrium,exists,dissolved,solute,und