S Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"16.2 ConcentraBons of SoluBons 1. Molarity -The concentraBon of a soluBon is a measure of the amount of solute dissolved in a given quanBty of solvent. Dilute soluCon: Contains a relaBvely small amount of solute. Concentrated soluBon: Contains a large amount of solute. Molarity (M): The number of moles of solute dissolved in one liter of soluBon. -Also known as molar concentraBon.   2. Making DiluBons DeﬁniCon: Reducing the number of moles of solute per unit volume by adding solvent, while the total number of moles of solute remains unchanged.","ConcentraBons,SoluBons,Molarity,concentraBon,soluBon,measure,amount,solute,dissolved,given,quanBty,solvent,Dilute,soluCon,Contains,relaBvely,small,amount,solute,Concentrated,soluBon,Contains,large,amount,solute,Molarity,number,moles,solute,dissolved,one,liter,soluBon,Also,known,molar,concentraBon,Making,DiluBons,De,niCon,Reducing,number,moles,solute,per,unit,volume,adding,solvent,total,number,moles,solute,remains,unchanged"
S Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"3. Percent SoluBons Percent by volume of a soluBon is the raBo of the volume of solute to the volume of soluBon.   Percent by mass of a soluBon is the raBo of the mass of the solute to the mass of the soluBon.   Summary: Three Ways to Calculate SoluBon ConcentraBon Molarity (M): Moles of solute per liter of soluBon. Percent by Volume (%(v/v)): Volume of solute per volume of soluBon × 100%. Percent by Mass (%(m/m)): Mass of solute per mass of soluBon × 100%. 16.3 ColligaBve ProperBes of SoluBons 1. Describing ColligaBve ProperBes ColligaCve ProperCes: ProperBes of soluBons that depend only on the number of solute parBcles, not their idenBty.  Three important colligaBve properBes:  -Vapor-pressure lowering -Freezing-point depression -Boiling-point elevaBon Eﬀect of solutes on vapor pressure:  Solute parBcles reduce the number of solvent parBcles able to escape into vapor. Equilibrium is established at a lower vapor pressure. Ionic vs. Molecular Solutes Ionic solutes that dissociate have a greater eﬀect on vapor pressure than nondissociaBng","Percent,SoluBons,Percent,volume,soluBon,raBo,volume,solute,volume,soluBon,Percent,mass,soluBon,raBo,mass,solute,mass,soluBon,Summary,Three,Ways,Calculate,SoluBon,ConcentraBon,Molarity,Moles,solute,per,liter,soluBon,Percent,Volume,v,v,Volume,solute,per,volume,soluBon,Percent,Mass,Mass,solute,per,mass,soluBon,ColligaBve,ProperBes,SoluBons,Describing,ColligaBve,ProperBes,ColligaCve,ProperCes,ProperBes,soluBons,depend,number,solute,parBcles,idenBty,Three,important,colligaBve,properBes,Vapor,pressure,lowering,Freezing,point,depression,Boiling,point,elevaBon,E,ect,solutes,vapor,pressure,Solute,parBcles,reduce,number,solvent,parBcles,able,escape,vapor,Equilibrium,established,lower,vapor,pressure,Ionic,vs,Molecular,Solutes,Ionic,solutes,dissociate,greater,e,ect,vapor,pressure,nondissociaBng"
S Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"solutes.  Example eﬀects of 3 moles of solute in water:  NaCl: 6 moles of parBcles (dissociates into 2 ions). CaCl₂: 9 moles of parBcles (dissociates into 3 ions). Glucose: 3 moles of parBcles (does not dissociate). The decrease in vapor pressure is proporBonal to the number of solute parBcles.  2. Freezing-Point Depression When a substance freezes, its parBcles form an orderly pa`ern. Eﬀect of solutes on freezing point:  -Solute disrupts the formaBon of the solid pa`ern. -More kineBc energy must be withdrawn for the soluBon to solidify. -The freezing point of a soluBon is lower than the freezing point of the pure solvent. Magnitude of freezing-point depression is proporBonal to the number of solute parBcles dissolved, not their idenBty. 3. Boiling-Point ElevaBon Boiling point: The temperature at which the vapor pressure of a liquid equals atmospheric pressure. Eﬀect of solutes on boiling point:  -Adding a nonvolaBle solute decreases the solvent’s vapor pressure. -More kineBc energy is required to raise the vapor pressure to atmospheric pressure, increasing the boiling point. -The boiling point of a soluBon is higher than the boiling point of the pure solvent. -sBll proporBonal 16.4 CalculaBons Involving ColligaBve ProperBes 1. Molality and Mole FracBon Molality is the number of moles of solute dissolved in 1 kilogram (1000 grams) of solvent.","solutes,Example,e,ects,moles,solute,water,NaCl,moles,parBcles,dissociates,ions,CaCl,moles,parBcles,dissociates,ions,Glucose,moles,parBcles,dissociate,decrease,vapor,pressure,proporBonal,number,solute,parBcles,Freezing,Point,Depression,substance,freezes,parBcles,form,orderly,pa,ern,E,ect,solutes,freezing,point,Solute,disrupts,formaBon,solid,pa,ern,kineBc,energy,must,withdrawn,soluBon,solidify,freezing,point,soluBon,lower,freezing,point,pure,solvent,Magnitude,freezing,point,depression,proporBonal,number,solute,parBcles,dissolved,idenBty,Boiling,Point,ElevaBon,Boiling,point,temperature,vap