AP Review Practice.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"elsewhere abroad. In turn, the American government threatened companies with imposing extra taxes for such a disadvantageous ac%vity.  Considering the EU, President Trump’s policy is mainly aimed at the German automobile industry/sector. Substan%al increasing of import tariﬀs for automobiles (BMW, Volkswagen and Daimler) will result in their price soaring in the American market, although they already have their own car factories there. Eventually, Germany and the network of car component producers in other EU countries will lose. If this happened, the EU would implement severe retalia%ons.18 American – Chinese clashes pertain to an immense trade surplus that has existed for decades since investors – not only Americans – transformed China into the “world factory”. However, the 18 It is being es%mated that Germany might lose 0.2% of GDP , and Poland 0.16% of GDP . The UE’s retalia%ons might induce an unemployment increase in the USA as much as about 4 million employees. The problem is more combined because many accusa%ons displayed by the American government against Chinese ac%vi%es in various ﬁelds aimed at reducing the increasing world power of the Chinese economy (i.e. joint ventures, illegal copying of new technologies and products, investments in IT sector). Finally, the USA has gradually been raising tariﬀs for Chinese merchandise and the lager country replied in the same way (the list of Chinese retalia%ons seems to be longer including ﬁnancial instruments and poli%cal ac%ons weakening sanc%ons against the North Korea). The problem remains since Chinese imports are s%ll dissa%sfactory.  Summing up, the American policy is not transparent because of its discrepancies in performance (e.g. breaking nego%a%ons of crea%on Trans-Atlan%c Trade and Investment Partnership – TTIP). Trade partners like the EU, Japan, or La%n America do not want to follow the US performance, principally as they expect advantages from American – Chinese trade conﬂicts.   Ques%ons 1. Discuss the possible short and long-run results of the introduc%on of protec%onism to interna%onal trade. 2. Graphically illustrate the impact of tariﬀ increases on imports of steel and aluminum to the US on: a) The US submarket of imported steel and aluminum, heavy industry and labor market b) markets (exporta%on sectors) in the following countries and their labor markets: • China • The EU 3. If the German automobile imports were burdened by much higher du%es than at present (25–30% instead of 2.5%) what countries would suﬀer?","elsewhere,abroad,turn,American,government,threatened,companies,imposing,extra,taxes,disadvantageous,ac,vity,Considering,EU,President,Trump,policy,mainly,aimed,German,automobile,industry,sector,Substan,al,increasing,import,tari,automobiles,BMW,Volkswagen,Daimler,result,price,soaring,American,market,although,already,car,factories,Eventually,Germany,network,car,component,producers,EU,countries,lose,happened,EU,would,implement,severe,retalia,ons,American,Chinese,clashes,pertain,immense,trade,surplus,existed,decades,since,investors,Americans,transformed,China,world,factory,However,es,mated,Germany,might,lose,GDP,Poland,GDP,UE,retalia,ons,might,induce,unemployment,increase,USA,much,million,employees,problem,combined,many,accusa,ons,displayed,American,government,Chinese,ac,vi,es,various,elds,aimed,reducing,increasing,world,power,Chinese,economy,e,joint,ventures,illegal,copying,new,technologies,products,investments,sector,Finally,USA,gradually,raising,tari,Chinese,merchandise,lager,country,replied,way,list,Chinese,retalia,ons,seems,longer,including,nancial,instruments,poli,cal,ac,ons,weakening,sanc,ons,North,Korea,problem,remains,since,Chinese,imports,dissa,sfactory,Summing,American,policy,transparent,discrepancies,performance,e,g,breaking,nego,ons,crea,Trans,Atlan,c,Trade,Investment,Partnership,TTIP,Trade,partners,like,EU,Japan,La,n,America,want,follow,US,performance,principally,expect,advantages,American,Chines