AP Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"✅ O’Brien’s metafictional layers suggest that the line between fiction and truth dissolves under trauma—not because memory fails, but because reality itself becomes unlivable. → The difference: one identifies a device; the other takes a stand.  2. Layer Interpretations, Not Just Evidence Go beyond “what this means” to “what this also could mean.” Look for interpretive friction—places where one image or moment can mean multiple things at once. Tension is where the complexity lives. Example:","Brien,metafictional,layers,suggest,line,fiction,truth,dissolves,trauma,memory,fails,reality,becomes,unlivable,difference,one,identifies,device,takes,stand,Layer,Interpretations,Evidence,Go,beyond,means,also,could,mean,Look,interpretive,friction,places,one,image,moment,mean,multiple,things,Tension,complexity,lives,Example"
AP Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Mary Anne’s transformation into a feral killer isn’t just a loss of innocence. It’s a metaphor for war’s erotic pull, the colonial fantasy of Vietnam, and the collapse of Western femininity under foreign pressure. → Ask: What’s beneath the obvious? What’s in conflict here?  3. Collapse the Distance Between Form and Meaning Don’t treat literary devices as separate from content. Instead, make your analysis show that the form is the content. •","Mary,Anne,transformation,feral,killer,loss,innocence,metaphor,war,erotic,pull,colonial,fantasy,Vietnam,collapse,Western,femininity,foreign,pressure,Ask,beneath,obvious,conflict,Collapse,Distance,Form,Meaning,treat,literary,devices,separate,content,Instead,make,analysis,show,form,content"
