H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Types of Relative Clauses  Relative clause:  -A clause that gives more information about a noun - Begins with who, which, that, where, whose, etc. Non-essential clause ( Nonrestrictive clause)  - A clause that gives more information also use comma - Example: ‘My brother, who lives in California, is visiting us next week’ Essential clause (restrictive clause):   - A clause that doesn’t give extra information   - No comma   - Example: The car that is parked outside belongs to my neighbour.  Parallel structure: —> definition: using the same grammatical form for similar                                                                                       elements in a sentence.  —> fixing parallel structure: Ensure each list or series use the same form. Example:'She likes reading, wriKng, and running.’ was easy (Not sure if this will be tested but you still need to know it): Journalistic voice: - inform audience about facts, events, issues                               - generally objective and neutral                               - concise and straightforward, sentences are often short.                               - follows inverted pyramid structure—> Important info        comes first.                               - heavily relies on quotes, statistics","Types,Relative,Clauses,Relative,clause,clause,gives,information,noun,Begins,whose,etc,Non,essential,clause,Nonrestrictive,clause,clause,gives,information,also,use,comma,Example,brother,lives,California,visiting,us,next,week,Essential,clause,restrictive,clause,clause,give,extra,information,comma,Example,car,parked,outside,belongs,neighbour,Parallel,structure,definition,using,grammatical,form,similar,elements,sentence,fixing,parallel,structure,Ensure,list,series,use,form,Example,likes,reading,wriKng,running,easy,sure,tested,still,need,know,Journalistic,voice,inform,audience,facts,events,issues,generally,objective,neutral,concise,straightforward,sentences,often,short,follows,inverted,pyramid,structure,Important,info,comes,first,heavily,relies,quotes,statistics"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Narrative voice: - aim to tell story                            - follows a narrative arc                            - subjective, reflective —> allowing emotional depth                            - focuses on character’s thoughts, feelings, motivations                            - more varied and flexible style—> sentences can be longer, more complex, often employing literary devices.                           - uses rich, evocative description to create atmosphere and evoke emotion.        Book of unknown Americans • When Written: 2009-2013  • When Published: 2014  • Genre: Literary fiction  • Setting: Newark, Delaware, U.S.A.  • Climax: Believing his daughter Maribel is in danger, Arturo  sets out to confront the boy he believes is responsible and he  is shot and killed along the way. Antagonist: Garrett Mille Plot summary:  The novel follows Arturo and Alma Rivera, who move from Mexico to Newark, Delaware with their teenage daughter Maribel in hopes of healing her after a traumatic brain injury. Maribel attends a special school while her parents struggle to adapt: Arturo works long hours at a mushroom farm, and Alma faces deep homesickness. Their lives intertwine with neighbors Celia and Rafael Toro, and their son Mayor, who becomes close to Maribel despite her condition. As Mayor and Maribel grow closer, they are targeted by a school bully, Garrett Miller, who eventually assaults Maribel. Alma tries to shield her daughter, but the incident and other tensions escalate. Arturo loses his job, and the family’s American dream begins to unravel. Meanwhile, Mayor defies his parents, steals","Narrative,voice,aim,tell,story,follows,narrative,arc,subjective,reflective,allowing,emotional,depth,focuses,character,thoughts,feelings,motivations,varied,flexible,style,sentences,longer,complex,often,employing,literary,devices,uses,rich,evocative,description,create,atmosphere,evoke,emotion,Book,unknown,Americans,Written,Published,Genre,Literary,fiction,Setting,Newark,Delaware,U,Climax,Believing,daughter,Maribel,danger,Arturo,sets,confront,boy,believes,responsible,shot,killed,along,way,Antagonist,Garrett,Mille,Plot,summary,novel,follows,Arturo,Alma,Rivera,move,Mexico,Newark,Delaware,teenage,daughter,Maribel,hopes,healing,traumatic,brain,injury,Maribel,attends,special,school,parents,struggle,adapt,Arturo,works,long,hours,mushroom,farm,Alma,faces,deep,homesickness,lives,intertwine,neighbors,Celia,Rafael,Toro,son,Mayor,becomes,close,Maribel,despite,condition,Mayor,Maribel,grow,closer,targeted,school,bully,Garrett,Miller,eventually,assaults,Maribel,Alma,tries,shield,daughter,incident,tensions,escalate,Arturo,loses,job,family,American,dream,begins,unravel,Meanwhile,Mayor,defies,parents,steals"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"a car, and takes Maribel out to experience her first snowfall, causing panic when she doesn’t return home. Arturo, trying to find Maribel, confronts Garrett’s family and is fatally shot by Garrett’s father. The tragedy devastates the community, and Alma and Maribel prepare to return to Mexico. The neighbors raise money to help them bring Arturo’s body back home. As Alma and Maribel leave, Alma realizes that Maribel, despite her injury, has always been herself, and they drive on, carrying their loss and hope into the future. Important characters:  1. Alma Rivera – The Protective and Guilt-Ridden Mother •Role: Protagonist, matriarch of the Rivera family. ••Themes: Isolation vs. community, maternal love, guilt, fear, adaptation. ••Character Arc: Alma begins the story consumed by guilt over her daughter’s accident, which propels her to seek a better life in the U.S. Though she finds community at the Redwood Apartments and builds deep friendships, especially with Celia, Alma remains emotionally isolated. Her overprotectiveness and silence about Garrett’s assault on Maribel—rooted in shame and fear—ultimately leads to tragic consequences. Yet, Alma also learns to reframe her understanding of Maribel, accepting her daughter not as “lost” but transformed. ••Symbolism: Alma symbolizes the sacrifices and quiet resilience of immigrant mothers, as well as the inner conflict between personal guilt and societal survival. •  2. Arturo Rivera – The Selfless Father and Martyr •Role: Breadwinner of the Rivera family. ••Themes: Sacrifice, family, the American Dream, dignity. •","car,takes,Maribel,experience,first,snowfall,causing,panic,return,home,Arturo,trying,find,Maribel,confronts,Garrett,family,fatally,shot,Garrett,father,tragedy,devastates,community,Alma,Maribel,prepare,return,Mexico,neighbors,raise,money,help,bring,Arturo,body,back,home,Alma,Maribel,leave,Alma,realizes,Maribel,despite,injury,always,drive,carrying,loss,hope,future,Important,characters,Alma,Rivera,Protective,Guilt,Ridden,Mother,Role,Protagonist,matriarch,Rivera,family,Themes,Isolation,vs,community,maternal,love,guilt,fear,adaptation,Character,Arc,Alma,begins,story,consumed,guilt,daughter,accident,propels,seek,better,life,U,Though,finds,community,Redwood,Apartments,builds,deep,friendships,especially,Celia,Alma,remains,emotionally,isolated,overprotectiveness,silence,Garrett,assault,Maribel,rooted,shame,fear,ultimately,leads,tragic,consequences,Yet,Alma,also,learns,reframe,understanding,Maribel,accepting,daughter,lost,transformed,Symbolism,Alma,symbolizes,sacrifices,quiet,resilience,immigrant,mothers,well,inner,conflict,personal,guilt,societal,survival,Arturo,Rivera,Selfless,Father,Martyr,Role,Breadwinner,Rivera,family,Themes,Sacrifice,family,American,Dream,dignity"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"•Character Arc: Arturo is hardworking and selfless, laboring in grim conditions to provide for Maribel’s education. His enduring optimism and humor provide emotional warmth to the family. However, his tragic death following a misunderstanding with Garrett's family positions him as a martyr—a man who dies trying to protect what little hope he had built. ••Symbolism: Arturo represents both the dream and the futility of the American immigrant experience—the idea that hard work doesn’t always yield justice or security in a broken system. •  3. Maribel Rivera – The Symbol of Lost and Reclaimed Identity •Role: Catalyst of the plot, daughter of Alma and Arturo. ••Themes: Disability, trauma, identity, love, coming of age. ••Character Arc: Once vibrant and rebellious, Maribel becomes quiet and confused after her injury. While many treat her as incapable, her relationship with Mayor reveals her inner self and potential for healing. By the end, she reclaims aspects of her identity, and Alma realizes she never truly lost her. ••Symbolism: Maribel embodies both the fragility and resilience of personhood. She challenges societal definitions of value and capability. •  4. Mayor Toro – The Sensitive Adolescent in Search of Purpose •Role: Alma’s narrator counterpart and Maribel’s friend/love interest. ••Themes: Adolescence, masculinity, love, rebellion, perception. •","Character,Arc,Arturo,hardworking,selfless,laboring,grim,conditions,provide,Maribel,education,enduring,optimism,humor,provide,emotional,warmth,family,However,tragic,death,following,misunderstanding,Garrett,family,positions,martyr,man,dies,trying,protect,little,hope,built,Symbolism,Arturo,represents,dream,futility,American,immigrant,experience,idea,hard,work,always,yield,justice,security,broken,system,Maribel,Rivera,Symbol,Lost,Reclaimed,Identity,Role,Catalyst,plot,daughter,Alma,Arturo,Themes,Disability,trauma,identity,love,coming,age,Character,Arc,vibrant,rebellious,Maribel,becomes,quiet,confused,injury,many,treat,incapable,relationship,Mayor,reveals,inner,self,potential,healing,end,reclaims,aspects,identity,Alma,realizes,never,truly,lost,Symbolism,Maribel,embodies,fragility,resilience,personhood,challenges,societal,definitions,value,capability,Mayor,Toro,Sensitive,Adolescent,Search,Purpose,Role,Alma,narrator,counterpart,Maribel,friend,love,interest,Themes,Adolescence,masculinity,love,rebellion,perception"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"•Character Arc: Mayor begins as a boy trapped by his father’s unrealistic expectations and his own self-doubt. His love for Maribel becomes a vehicle for asserting his identity, but also exposes his immaturity and naivety. His idealized love leads to tension and tragedy, but also growth. ••Symbolism: Mayor represents the second-generation immigrant youth, caught between two cultures and expectations, struggling to find his own path. •  5. Celia Toro – The Bridge Between Cultures •Role: Mayor’s mother, Alma’s best friend. ••Themes: Friendship, gender roles, nostalgia, endurance. ••Character Arc: Celia is nurturing and pragmatic, supporting her family and friends with wisdom and kindness. She balances emotional intelligence with practicality, especially in her friendship with Alma and in handling her husband Rafael’s temperament. She also symbolizes the longing for home—one that no longer exists. ••Symbolism: Celia is the emotional bedrock of the community and an anchor to the immigrant experience of lost roots and found kinship. •  6. Rafael ""Rafa"" Toro – The Disillusioned Patriarch •Role: Mayor’s father. ••Themes: Masculinity, authority, trauma, generational conflict. ••","Character,Arc,Mayor,begins,boy,trapped,father,unrealistic,expectations,self,doubt,love,Maribel,becomes,vehicle,asserting,identity,also,exposes,immaturity,naivety,idealized,love,leads,tension,tragedy,also,growth,Symbolism,Mayor,represents,second,generation,immigrant,youth,caught,two,cultures,expectations,struggling,find,path,Celia,Toro,Bridge,Cultures,Role,Mayor,mother,Alma,best,friend,Themes,Friendship,gender,roles,nostalgia,endurance,Character,Arc,Celia,nurturing,pragmatic,supporting,family,friends,wisdom,kindness,balances,emotional,intelligence,practicality,especially,friendship,Alma,handling,husband,Rafael,temperament,also,symbolizes,longing,home,one,longer,exists,Symbolism,Celia,emotional,bedrock,community,anchor,immigrant,experience,lost,roots,found,kinship,Rafael,Rafa,Toro,Disillusioned,Patriarch,Role,Mayor,father,Themes,Masculinity,authority,trauma,generational,conflict"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Character Arc: Rafa’s harshness comes from a desire to see his family thrive, shaped by his traumatic past. His rigidity and obsession with success create conflict, especially with Mayor. He embodies the difficulty many first-generation immigrants face when reconciling past hardship with a new culture. ••Symbolism: Rafa symbolizes both the trauma of war and migration, and the dangers of unyielding patriarchal expectations. •  7. Garrett Miller – The Embodiment of Threat and Privilege •Role: Local bully and antagonist. ••Themes: Racism, privilege, danger, repression. ••Character Arc: Garrett is mostly static—he represents the looming threat to immigrant safety. His harassment of Maribel and subsequent confrontation with Arturo result in tragedy. Yet, glimpses of his own troubled family life suggest a cycle of abuse and neglect. ••Symbolism: Garrett personifies unchecked privilege, toxic masculinity, and the dangers of silence and complicity. •  8. Quisqueya Solis – The Masked Survivor •Role: Gossip and narrator of her own story. ••Themes: Shame, survival, identity. ••","Character,Arc,Rafa,harshness,comes,desire,see,family,thrive,shaped,traumatic,past,rigidity,obsession,success,create,conflict,especially,Mayor,embodies,difficulty,many,first,generation,immigrants,face,reconciling,past,hardship,new,culture,Symbolism,Rafa,symbolizes,trauma,war,migration,dangers,unyielding,patriarchal,expectations,Garrett,Miller,Embodiment,Threat,Privilege,Role,Local,bully,antagonist,Themes,Racism,privilege,danger,repression,Character,Arc,Garrett,mostly,static,represents,looming,threat,immigrant,safety,harassment,Maribel,subsequent,confrontation,Arturo,result,tragedy,Yet,glimpses,troubled,family,life,suggest,cycle,abuse,neglect,Symbolism,Garrett,personifies,unchecked,privilege,toxic,masculinity,dangers,silence,complicity,Quisqueya,Solis,Masked,Survivor,Role,Gossip,narrator,story,Themes,Shame,survival,identity"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Character Arc: Outwardly flamboyant and dramatic, Quisqueya hides a history of abuse and trauma. Her need for control and social status masks a painful past. ••Symbolism: She represents the complexities of identity formation and the need to rewrite one’s narrative to survive. •  Supporting Characters as Community Voices: Each minor character at Redwood Apartments adds texture to the immigrant mosaic: •Benny Quinto – A former seminarian turned smuggler and prayer leader; symbolizes contradiction and redemption. ••Gustavo Milhojas – A janitor with a tragic past and enduring hope; reflects perseverance and generational sacrifice. ••Adolfo “Fito” Angelino – The kind landlord; symbolizes the evolution of the American Dream. ••Nelia Zafón & José Mercado – Artists thwarted by systems of oppression; embody the theme of unrealized potential. ••Micho Alvarez – A photographer and activist; voices political resistance and cultural pride. •","Character,Arc,Outwardly,flamboyant,dramatic,Quisqueya,hides,history,abuse,trauma,need,control,social,status,masks,painful,past,Symbolism,represents,complexities,identity,formation,need,rewrite,one,narrative,survive,Supporting,Characters,Community,Voices,minor,character,Redwood,Apartments,adds,texture,immigrant,mosaic,Benny,Quinto,former,seminarian,turned,smuggler,prayer,leader,symbolizes,contradiction,redemption,Gustavo,Milhojas,janitor,tragic,past,enduring,hope,reflects,perseverance,generational,sacrifice,Adolfo,Fito,Angelino,kind,landlord,symbolizes,evolution,American,Dream,Nelia,Zaf,n,Jos,Mercado,Artists,thwarted,systems,oppression,embody,theme,unrealized,potential,Micho,Alvarez,photographer,activist,voices,political,resistance,cultural,pride"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Key themes: 1. Immigration and the American Dream •Explored through: Nearly every character, particularly Alma and Arturo Rivera. ••Many characters came to the U.S. seeking opportunity, safety, or a better future for their children. Yet the reality they face—poor working conditions, poverty, and social alienation—shows the complexity and sometimes the futility of the ""American Dream."" ••Arturo's tragic death underscores how even hardworking, optimistic immigrants can be thwarted by systemic barriers and prejudice. •  2. Isolation vs. Community •Explored through: Alma Rivera, Celia Toro, the Redwood Apartments residents. ••The immigrants are isolated by language, culture, and trauma. Alma feels especially alone due to guilt and fear. ••However, the Redwood Apartments become a small, vibrant community where immigrants support one another in ways that are emotionally and materially vital. •  3. Love and Relationships •Explored through: Mayor and Maribel’s budding romance; Alma and Arturo's marriage; the Toro family dynamics. ••","Key,themes,Immigration,American,Dream,Explored,Nearly,every,character,particularly,Alma,Arturo,Rivera,Many,characters,came,U,seeking,opportunity,safety,better,future,children,Yet,reality,face,poor,working,conditions,poverty,social,alienation,shows,complexity,sometimes,futility,American,Dream,Arturo,tragic,death,underscores,even,hardworking,optimistic,immigrants,thwarted,systemic,barriers,prejudice,Isolation,vs,Community,Explored,Alma,Rivera,Celia,Toro,Redwood,Apartments,residents,immigrants,isolated,language,culture,trauma,Alma,feels,especially,alone,due,guilt,fear,However,Redwood,Apartments,become,small,vibrant,community,immigrants,support,one,another,ways,emotionally,materially,vital,Love,Relationships,Explored,Mayor,Maribel,budding,romance,Alma,Arturo,marriage,Toro,family,dynamics"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Love is shown in its many forms: romantic, parental, platonic. The novel questions what it means to love someone who is changed by trauma, as Alma does with Maribel. ••Mayor’s love for Maribel becomes both redemptive and problematic—raising ethical questions about consent, care, and agency. •  4. Trauma and Recovery •Explored through: Maribel’s brain injury; Quisqueya’s childhood abuse; immigrants' stories of war, loss, and displacement. ••The characters carry emotional and physical scars. Maribel’s recovery is slow and nonlinear, and each immigrant’s trauma affects how they integrate into American society. ••The novel suggests that healing is possible, but it requires patience, understanding, and community. •  5. Family and Sacrifice •Explored through: Alma and Arturo’s sacrifices for Maribel; Rafa’s demands of his sons; Celia’s longing for her family. ••Immigrant parents often sacrifice everything to give their children better lives, even at great personal cost. ••Alma’s guilt and resolve to help Maribel drive the family's migration, and Arturo ultimately pays with his life. •  6. Identity and Belonging","Love,shown,many,forms,romantic,parental,platonic,novel,questions,means,love,someone,changed,trauma,Alma,Maribel,Mayor,love,Maribel,becomes,redemptive,problematic,raising,ethical,questions,consent,care,agency,Trauma,Recovery,Explored,Maribel,brain,injury,Quisqueya,childhood,abuse,immigrants,stories,war,loss,displacement,characters,carry,emotional,physical,scars,Maribel,recovery,slow,nonlinear,immigrant,trauma,affects,integrate,American,society,novel,suggests,healing,possible,requires,patience,understanding,community,Family,Sacrifice,Explored,Alma,Arturo,sacrifices,Maribel,Rafa,demands,sons,Celia,longing,family,Immigrant,parents,often,sacrifice,everything,give,children,better,lives,even,great,personal,cost,Alma,guilt,resolve,help,Maribel,drive,family,migration,Arturo,ultimately,pays,life,Identity,Belonging"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"•Explored through: Mayor’s adolescent insecurities; Rafa’s rejection of his “gringo” label; Celia’s nostalgia for Panamá. Lexis: Voice - The distinct personality or style of an  author as expressed in their writing - The author's  voice in the novel captures the struggles of  adolescence with humor and poignancy.  Auto theory - A concept that emphasizes self reflection and personal experience in understanding  art and literature - In her essay, she applied auto  theory to explore how her own upbringing influenced  her interpretation of the text.  Assimilation - The process by which individuals  or groups adopt the cultural norms of another  group, often losing their original identity – The  story explores the challenges of assimilation  faced by immigrant families in a new country. Cultural monolith - The idea that a culture is uniform and unchanging, ignoring its diversity and complexity - The film critiques the concept of a cultural monolith by showcasing the varied experiences within a single community.  Passing (for white) - The act of a person of non-white ethnicity being  perceived as white, often to gain social advantages - The protagonist  grapples with the implications of passing for white in a society that  values whiteness.  Point of view -The perspective from  which a story is told, influencing how","Explored,Mayor,adolescent,insecurities,Rafa,rejection,gringo,label,Celia,nostalgia,Panam,Lexis,Voice,distinct,personality,style,author,expressed,writing,author,voice,novel,captures,struggles,adolescence,humor,poignancy,Auto,theory,concept,emphasizes,self,reflection,personal,experience,understanding,art,literature,essay,applied,auto,theory,explore,upbringing,influenced,interpretation,text,Assimilation,process,individuals,groups,adopt,cultural,norms,another,group,often,losing,original,identity,story,explores,challenges,assimilation,faced,immigrant,families,new,country,Cultural,monolith,idea,culture,uniform,unchanging,ignoring,diversity,complexity,film,critiques,concept,cultural,monolith,showcasing,varied,experiences,within,single,community,Passing,white,act,person,non,white,ethnicity,perceived,white,often,gain,social,advantages,protagonist,grapples,implications,passing,white,society,values,whiteness,Point,view,perspective,story,told,influencing"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"events and characters are perceived - The shift in point of view in the novel  allows readers to understand the motivations of both the hero and the  antagonist.  Double consciousness -A concept describing the internal conflict  experienced by marginalized groups in a dominant culture, particularly  in understanding one's identity - The character's sense of double  consciousness highlights the struggle between their cultural heritage  and societal expectations. Latino -A term used to describe people of Latin American origin or descent, encompassing a diverse range of cultures and identities - The anthology features stories from various Latino authors, each offering unique perspectives on  identity and belonging. Vocab Surge - A sudden and powerful increase or rise  - I lay there listening to the soft chorus of Maribel’s  and Arturo’s long, even breaths. In and out. In and  out. The surge of possibility. The tug of doubt.  Had we done the right thing, coming here?  Ambivalent - Experiencing mixed feelings; often  feelings of both liking and disliking - When he looked  ambivalent, I lied and said, “I’ve been practicing  with my dad at home. Even he thinks I’m getting  better.”  Bewildered - confused – It was a story about  how Yu Li came to the United States from China  with his parents. He went to school one  day and some of the kids taunted him and  some of them were kind. But Yu Li didn’t know","events,characters,perceived,shift,point,view,novel,allows,readers,understand,motivations,hero,antagonist,Double,consciousness,concept,describing,internal,conflict,experienced,marginalized,groups,dominant,culture,particularly,understanding,one,identity,character,sense,double,consciousness,highlights,struggle,cultural,heritage,societal,expectations,Latino,term,used,describe,people,Latin,American,origin,descent,encompassing,diverse,range,cultures,identities,anthology,features,stories,various,Latino,authors,offering,unique,perspectives,identity,belonging,Vocab,Surge,sudden,powerful,increase,rise,lay,listening,soft,chorus,Maribel,Arturo,long,even,breaths,surge,possibility,tug,doubt,done,right,thing,coming,Ambivalent,Experiencing,mixed,feelings,often,feelings,liking,disliking,looked,ambivalent,lied,said,practicing,dad,home,Even,thinks,getting,better,Bewildered,confused,story,Yu,Li,came,United,States,China,parents,went,school,one,day,kids,taunted,kind,Yu,Li,know"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"English, so he was bewildered. Meagerness - a small amount; not  enough - But when Arturo didn’t say  anything, I felt acutely the meagerness of  it, the insufficiency. We wanted more. We  wanted what we had come here for.  Compliant - Willing to follow rules, standards, or requests - She  resisted, she was confused, and then, suddenly, something would  snap back into place and she was compliant, agreeable.  Imploring -Begging or pleading  earnestly and desperately - And  sometimes I went to the small church  we had found, St. Thomas More  Oratory, with its water-stained drop  ceiling and its folding chairs in place of  pews, and sat alone in the empty  sanctuary, reciting the same prayers  over and over, imploring God to  listen.  Indignation - Strong displeasure or anger aroused by something seen  as unfair - The boy said something in English, something unintelligible  to me, but I could hear the indignation in his tone, and without  thinking, I turned and spat in his face. Conception - process of conceiving or  forming an idea; the beginning or origin of  something - Arturo usually frowned when  she did those things—they didn’t fit the  Mexican conception of what girls could and","English,bewildered,Meagerness,small,amount,enough,Arturo,say,anything,felt,acutely,meagerness,insufficiency,wanted,wanted,come,Compliant,Willing,follow,rules,standards,requests,resisted,confused,suddenly,something,would,snap,back,place,compliant,agreeable,Imploring,Begging,pleading,earnestly,desperately,sometimes,went,small,church,found,St,Thomas,Oratory,water,stained,drop,ceiling,folding,chairs,place,pews,sat,alone,empty,sanctuary,reciting,prayers,imploring,God,listen,Indignation,Strong,displeasure,anger,aroused,something,seen,unfair,boy,said,something,English,something,unintelligible,could,hear,indignation,tone,without,thinking,turned,spat,face,Conception,process,conceiving,forming,idea,beginning,origin,something,Arturo,usually,frowned,things,fit,Mexican,conception,girls,could"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"should do—but I loved that about Maribel.  Reproach - Expressing disapproval - “I’m sorry,” I murmured, the  combined weight of horror and reproach pressing against my chest.  Rehabilitation - The process of  restoring someone to health - After  weeks of rehabilitation, after  working with a psychologist and a  speech language pathologist and the  doctor, all they could tell us was  things like: She struggles with finding  the right words sometimes, and that  will likely persist.  Machismo - A strong or exaggerated sense of masculinity, often  characterized by dominance - In the time that he and my mom had  been gone, I’d talked myself into the idea that maybe my dad would  be proud of me—just a little bit—when he found out I’d gotten into a  fight. Maybe it proved I had a little bit of machismo in me after all. Indestructible - Impossible to destroy or  damage - We believed we were special. We  believed we were indestructible. But after  the accident, under the gathering clouds of  fate, something changed.  Careened -to fall into someone or something; out of control- He  careened around us and, as he passed by, yelled out his open  window, “Learn how to f**king drive!”  Lusted - a strong desire for someone or  something - I had a feeling she’d been  one of the popular girls, the one all","loved,Maribel,Reproach,Expressing,disapproval,sorry,murmured,combined,weight,horror,reproach,pressing,chest,Rehabilitation,process,restoring,someone,health,weeks,rehabilitation,working,psychologist,speech,language,pathologist,doctor,could,tell,us,things,like,struggles,finding,right,words,sometimes,likely,persist,Machismo,strong,exaggerated,sense,masculinity,often,characterized,dominance,time,mom,gone,talked,idea,maybe,dad,would,proud,little,bit,found,gotten,fight,Maybe,proved,little,bit,machismo,Indestructible,Impossible,destroy,damage,believed,special,believed,indestructible,accident,gathering,clouds,fate,something,changed,Careened,fall,som