H+ Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"•Telephone: story begins with the phone call, the start of Quinn aWempDng to create meaning in his own life; second phone call was from Auster where he reveals SDllman Sr.’s suicide, destroys Quinn’s newfound purpose. Marks the end aand the start as a callback •Hand/Pen: The pen, ﬁrst sold to Quinn by a deaf man with the note ""Learn to Speak to Your Friends,"" becomes a symbol of self-expression. As Quinn opens up, his wriDng grows more subjecDve—seen in his reﬂecDons on homelessness (pp. 102–104), where the handwriDng shi`s into his own and the narraDon becomes philosophical. On pp. 88–89, the pen’s direcDon mirrors his emoDonal state—poised to record Auster’s insight, then stunned and inacDve when expectaDons collapse. By pp. 130–131, Quinn is visually submerged, beginning with his pen, symbolizing how his idenDty dissolves with his loss of wriDng. His ability to express is his sense of self—once wriDng fails, so does he. •Fingerprint: used to idenDfy people, used in the form of a maze in the novel; symbolizes how Quinn feels lost no maWer where he is – his confusion with his idenDty and existenDal meaning •Eggs: appears iniDally along with humpty dumpty analogy; SDllman believes people must be “broken” before achieving greater meaning – may also correlate to Quinn’s mental fragility especially a`er Auster’s phone call noDfying him about SDllman’s death •Max Work: protagonist of William Wilson’s novel; the detecDve idenDty that Quinn envisioned himself being – Quinn has shaped himself through the lens of Max Work","Telephone,story,begins,phone,call,start,Quinn,aWempDng,create,meaning,life,second,phone,call,Auster,reveals,SDllman,Sr,suicide,destroys,Quinn,newfound,purpose,Marks,end,aand,start,callback,Hand,Pen,pen,rst,sold,Quinn,deaf,man,note,Learn,Speak,Friends,becomes,symbol,self,expression,Quinn,opens,wriDng,grows,subjecDve,seen,ecDons,homelessness,pp,handwriDng,shi,narraDon,becomes,philosophical,pp,pen,direcDon,mirrors,