H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"FactorEffect on Demographic MomentumLarge young populationHigh demographic momentum, continued growthSmall young populationLow demographic momentum, possible population declineDeclining fertilityGrowth slows, but momentum may keep population rising for yearsStable/aging populationLittle or no demographic momentum, possible decline1.5Demographic Graph Demographic momentum (also called population momentum) is the tendency for a population to continue growing for some time after fertility rates have declined to replacement level, due to the existing age structure of the population.","FactorEffect,Demographic,MomentumLarge,young,populationHigh,demographic,momentum,continued,growthSmall,young,populationLow,demographic,momentum,possible,population,declineDeclining,fertilityGrowth,slows,momentum,may,keep,population,rising,yearsStable,aging,populationLittle,demographic,momentum,possible,decline,Demographic,Graph,Demographic,momentum,also,called,population,momentum,tendency,population,continue,growing,time,fertility,rates,declined,replacement,level,due,existing,age,structure,population"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"1.6Maternal Mortality Ratio Which ratio is the greatest health disparity between LDCs & MDCs? CBR, CDR, Or TFR?Answer: Maternal mortality ratio (*/100,000 live births)1.7What is the Dependency Ratio? The dependency ratio measures the proportion of non-working-age people (children and elderly) to working-age people in a population, highlighting the potential economic and social support burden on the productive segment of society. High Dependency Ratio: Indicates a larger proportion of dependents, meaning more economic pressure on the working-age population to support the young and elderly through taxes, social security, and other means.Low Dependency Ratio: Suggests fewer dependents per worker, potentially allowing for greater investment in economic growth, savings, or improved social services.1.8What is the Elderly Dependency Ratio? The elderly dependency ratio (also called the old-age dependency ratio) measures the proportion of elderly people—typically those aged 65 and older—relative to the working-age population, usually defined as those aged 15 to 64. This ratio indicates the potential economic burden on the working-age population to support the elderly, who are generally retired and economically inactive. High elderly dependency ratio: Indicates a greater burden on the working-age population to provide for pensions, healthcare, and other needs of the elderly.Low elderly dependency ratio: Suggests a smaller proportion of elderly dependents, meaning less pressure on the working-age population.","Maternal,Mortality,Ratio,ratio,greatest,health,disparity,LDCs,MDCs,CBR,CDR,TFR,Answer,Maternal,mortality,ratio,live,births,Dependency,Ratio,dependency,ratio,measures,proportion,non,working,age,people,children,elderly,working,age,people,population,highlighting,potential,economic,social,support,burden,productive,segment,society,High,Dependency,Ratio,Indicates,larger,proportion,dependents,meaning,economic,pressure,working,age,population,support,young,elderly,taxes,social,security,means,Low,Dependency,Ratio,Suggests,fewer,dependents,per,worker,potentially,allowing,greater,investment,economic,growth,savings,improved,social,services,Elderly,Dependency,Ratio,elderly,dependency,ratio,also,called,old,age,dependency,ratio,measures,proportion,elderly,people,typically,aged,older,relative,working,age,population,usually,defined,aged,ratio,indicates,potential,economic,burden,working,age,population,support,elderly,generally,retired,economically,inactive,High,elderly,dependency,ratio,Indicates,greater,burden,working,age,population,provide,pensions,healthcare,needs,elderly,Low,elderly,dependency,ratio,Suggests,smaller,proportion,elderly,dependents,meaning,less,pressure,working,age,population"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"StageNameBirthRateDeathRatePopulationGrowthExample Countries/Regions1High StationaryHighHighStable or slowPre-industrial societies (e.g., 18th-centuryEurope)2EarlyExpandingHighFallingRapid increaseNiger, Afghanistan, Sub-Saharan Africa3Late ExpandingFallingLowSlowing growthIndia, Brazil, Mexico4Low StationaryLowLowStable or slowUSA, France, South Korea1.9What is the Youth Dependency Ratio? The youth dependency ratio measures the number of young people (aged 0–14) relative to the working-age population (aged 15–64). It indicates the economic and social burden on the working-age population to support children through education, healthcare, and other services. Highlights the pressure on resources for childcare, education, and family support systems. A higher ratio means more children depend on each working-age person.1.10Four Stages of the Demographic Transition Model (DTM) The Demographic Transition Model (DTM) describes how birth and death rates shift as societies industrialize, leading to population changes. Here are the four stages:","StageNameBirthRateDeathRatePopulationGrowthExample,Countries,Regions,High,StationaryHighHighStable,slowPre,industrial,societies,e,g,th,centuryEurope,EarlyExpandingHighFallingRapid,increaseNiger,Afghanistan,Sub,Saharan,Africa,Late,ExpandingFallingLowSlowing,growthIndia,Brazil,Mexico,Low,StationaryLowLowStable,slowUSA,France,South,Korea,Youth,Dependency,Ratio,youth,dependency,ratio,measures,number,young,people,aged,relative,working,age,population,aged,indicates,economic,social,burden,working,age,population,support,children,education,healthcare,services,Highlights,pressure,resources,childcare,education,family,support,systems,higher,ratio,means,children,depend,working,age,person,Four,Stages,Demographic,Transition,Model,DTM,Demographic,Transition,Model,DTM,describes,birth,death,rates,shift,societies,industrialize,leading,population,changes,four,stages"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"1.11Key Assumptions of the DTM: 1. Linear progression: All countries follow the same path from Stage 1 to 4 as they develop economically.2. Industrialization-driven: Declining death/birth rates are tied to technological and social advancements (e.g., healthcare, education).3. No migration impact: The model ignores migration’s role in population changes.1.12Stage 5? Some scholars propose Stage 5 (post-industrial decline), where:  Birth rates fall below death rates, causing a natural decrease in population.  Examples: Japan, Germany, Italy, where death rates exceed births, but total population is often maintained by immigration.  Criticism: The DTM doesn’t account for migration, so Stage 5 remains debated.1.13Relating DTM to Population Pyramid Each DTM stage corresponds to distinct population pyramid shapes:","Key,Assumptions,DTM,Linear,progression,countries,follow,path,Stage,develop,economically,Industrialization,driven,Declining,death,birth,rates,tied,technological,social,advancements,e,g,healthcare,education,migration,impact,model,ignores,migration,role,population,changes,Stage,scholars,propose,Stage,post,industrial,decline,Birth,rates,fall,death,rates,causing,natural,decrease,population,Examples,Japan,Germany,Italy,death,rates,exceed,births,total,population,often,maintained,immigration,Criticism,DTM,account,migration,Stage,remains,debated,Relating,DTM,Population,Pyramid,DTM,stage,corresponds,distinct,population,pyramid,shapes"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"DTM StagePopulation Pyramid ShapeCharacteristics1Wide base, narrow topHigh youth dependency; short life expectancy2TriangularYouth bulge due to falling death rates3Bell-shapedNarrowing base as birth rates decline4Rectangular/columnarBalanced age groups; aging population5Inverted pyramidMore elderly than youth; shrinking workforce","DTM,StagePopulation,Pyramid,ShapeCharacteristics,Wide,base,narrow,topHigh,youth,dependency,short,life,expectancy,TriangularYouth,bulge,due,falling,death,rates,Bell,shapedNarrowing,base,birth,rates,decline,Rectangular,columnarBalanced,age,groups,aging,population,Inverted,pyramidMore,elderly,youth,shrinking,workforce"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"StageName/DescriptionCrudeBirth Rate(CBR)CrudeDeath Rate(CDR)NaturalIncrease Rate(NIR)Causes/CharacteristicsExampleCountries/Regions1High FluctuatingLow GrowthHigh (40–50 /1,000)High (40–50 /1,000)None (0 / 1,000)Lack of family planning, high infant mortality,need for farm workers, disease, famine, warBritain prior to 1750s2Early ExpandingHigh GrowthHigh (40+ /1,000)Lower (15 /1,000)High (25+ /1,000)Improved sanitation, food production/storage,medical advances, lower infant mortalityBritain 1750s–late 19th c.;Bangladesh, Nigeria today3Late ExpandingDecreasing GrowthLower (20 /1,000)Low (10 /1,000)Low (10 /1,000)Higher living standards, better healthcare,education, family planning, mechanization,pensionsBritain late 19th c.; China,Brazil today4Low FluctuatingLow GrowthLow (10 /1,000)Low (10 /1,000)None (0 / 1,000)Settled economy, developed middle class, politicalstability, zero population growthUSA, Sweden, Japan,Britain today1.14Malthus's Theory Thomas Malthus argued in 1798 that:  Population grows exponentially (e.g., 2, 4, 8, 16...).  Food supply grows arithmetically (e.g., 2, 4, 6, 8...).  This imbalance leads to ""positive checks"" (famine, disease) and ""preventative checks"" (late marriage).  Criticism:  Technological advances (e.g., Green Revolution) disproved Malthus’s food scarcity predictions.  Ignores global trade and family planning’s role in population control.  1.15Four Stages","StageName,DescriptionCrudeBirth,Rate,CBR,CrudeDeath,Rate,CDR,NaturalIncrease,Rate,NIR,Causes,CharacteristicsExampleCountries,Regions,High,FluctuatingLow,GrowthHigh,High,None,Lack,family,planning,high,infant,mortality,need,farm,workers,disease,famine,warBritain,prior,Early,ExpandingHigh,GrowthHigh,Lower,High,Improved,sanitation,food,production,storage,medical,advances,lower,infant,mortalityBritain,late,th,c,Bangladesh,Nigeria,today,Late,ExpandingDecreasing,GrowthLower,Low,Low,Higher,living,standards,better,healthcare,education,family,planning,mechanization,pensionsBritain,late,th,c,China,Brazil,today,Low,FluctuatingLow,GrowthLow,Low,None,Settled,economy,developed,middle,class,politicalstability,zero,population,growthUSA,Sweden,Japan,Britain,today,Malthus,Theory,Thomas,Malthus,argued,Population,grows,exponentially,e,g,Food,supply,grows,arithmetically,e,g,imbalance,leads,positive,checks,famine,disease,preventative,checks,late,marriage,Criticism,Technological,advances,e,g,Green,Revolution,disproved,Malthus,food,scarcity,predictions,Ignores,global,trade,family,planning,role,population,control,Four,Stages"
