H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"LawNo.Law/PrincipleExplanation1Most migration is over a short distance.Migrants tend to move short distances, often within their own country,rather than across great distances.2Migration occurs in steps.Migration is not usually a single, direct move to a far-off destination.Instead, people often move in a series of shorter steps, gradually gettingcloser to their ultimate goal (e.g., from a village to a town, then to a city).3Long-range migrants usually move tourban areas.When people do migrate long distances, they tend to head towards majorcenters of commerce and industry (cities) where economic opportunities areperceived to be greater.4Each migration flow produces acompensating counter-flow.For every stream of migration in one direction, there is usually a smaller,reverse flow of migrants moving back or in the opposite direction (e.g.,return migration, people leaving cities).5Rural dwellers are more migratory thanurban dwellers.People living in rural areas are generally more likely to migrate than thosealready living in urban areas, often seeking better opportunities in cities.6Within their own country, females aremore migratory than males, but malesare more migratory over long distances.Women tend to migrate more within their own country (often for reasonslike family, marriage, or domestic work), while men are more likely toembark on long-distance or international migrations.7Most migrants are adults.The majority of migrants are young adults, often seeking employment oreducational opportunities.8Large towns grow more by migrationthan by natural increase.Major urban centers experience significant population growth due to theinflux of migrants rather than solely from births exceeding deaths.9Migration increases with economicdevelopment.As a country develops economically, internal migration (and ofteninternational migration) tends to increase as people seek out newopportunities.10Migration is mostly due to economiccauses.The primary driver for most migration is economic factors, such as thesearch for better jobs, higher wages, or improved living conditions.MigrationTypeDefinitionKey Drivers/ReasonsInternationalMovement across national borders.Conflict, persecution, economic opportunity, better livingstandards, political stability, regional agreements.InterregionalMovement from one major region of a country toanother major region within the same country.Economic opportunities, climate, lower cost of living,government policy, industrialization.IntraregionalMovement within a single region of a country (e.g.,within a metropolitan area, or within a specificstate/province).Desire for larger homes, better schools, lower crime,quieter lifestyle, remote work, changing life stages,housing affordability.2.2Real-World Migration","LawNo,Law,PrincipleExplanation,migration,short,distance,Migrants,tend,move,short,distances,often,within,country,rather,across,great,distances,Migration,occurs,steps,Migration,usually,single,direct,move,far,destination,Instead,people,often,move,series,shorter,steps,gradually,gettingcloser,ultimate,goal,e,g,village,town,city,Long,range,migrants,usually,move,tourban,areas,people,migrate,long,distances,tend,head,towards,majorcenters,commerce,industry,cities,economic,opportunities,areperceived,greater,migration,flow,produces,acompensating,counter,flow,every,stream,migration,one,direction,usually,smaller,reverse,flow,migrants,moving,back,opposite,direction,e,g,return,migration,people,leaving,cities,Rural,dwellers,migratory,thanurban,dwellers,People,living,rural,areas,generally,likely,migrate,thosealready,living,urban,areas,often,seeking,better,opportunities,cities,Within,country,females,aremore,migratory,males,malesare,migratory,long,distances,Women,tend,migrate,within,country,often,reasonslike,family,marriage,domestic,work,men,likely,toembark,long,distance,international,migrations,migrants,adults,majority,migrants,young,adults,often,seeking,employment,oreducational,opportunities,Large,towns,grow,migrationthan,natural,increase,Major,urban,centers,experience,significant,population,growth,due,theinflux,migrants,rather,solely,births,exceeding,deaths,Migration,increases,economicdevelopment,country,develops,economically,internal,migration,ofteninternational,migration,tends,increase,people,seek,newopportunities,Migration,mostly,due,economiccauses,primary,driver,migration,economic,factors,thesearch,better,jobs,higher,wages,improved,living,conditions,MigrationTypeDefinitionKey,Drivers,ReasonsInternationalMovement,across,national,borders,Conflict,persecution,economic,opportunity,better,livingstandards,political,stability,regional,agreements,InterregionalMovement,one,major,region,country,toanother,major,region,within,country,Economic,opportunities,climate,lower,cost,living,government,policy,industrialization,IntraregionalMovement,within,single,region,country,e,g,within,metropolitan,area,within,specificstate,province,Desire,larger,homes,better,schools,lower,crime,quieter,lifestyle,remote,work,changing,life,stages,housing,affordability,Real,World,Migration"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Chain migration is when immigrants follow others from their home area to a new place, often based on family or social connections. This can lead to communities of people from the same origin forming in the new location.","Chain,migration,immigrants,follow,others,home,area,new,place,often,based,family,social,connections,lead,communities,people,origin,forming,new,location"
H Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"FactorCategoryDescriptionPush Factors (Reasons to Leave)Pull Factors (Reasons to Come)E - EconomicMoney, jobs,financialopportunities,standard of living.High unemployment, low wages, poverty, lack ofeconomic opportunity, high cost of living.Higher wages, better job prospects,economic growth, lower cost ofliving, educational/trainingopportunities.S - SocialCulture, family,community,discrimination,social services,quality of life.Discrimination, persecution (religious/ethnic), lackof social services (healthcare, education), familyseparation, limited social freedoms.Presence of family/friends (chainmigration), cultural affinity, betteraccess to social services, greatersocial freedoms/acceptance, betterquality of life.P - PoliticalGovernment,stability, conflict,human rights,safety.War, political instability, persecution by government,lack of human rights, corruption, forceddisplacement, lack of safety/security.Political stability, safety and security,protection of human rights, goodgovernance, asylum opportunities,freedom from conflict.N -EnvironmentalNatural world,climate,resources,disasters.Natural disasters (floods, droughts, earthquakes),climate change impacts (sea-level rise,desertification), resource scarcity (water, arableland), pollution, environmental degradation makingan area uninhabitable.Safer environment, more fertile land,reliable water sources, less exposureto natural hazards, cleanerenvironment.Medical brain drain is the emigration of trained healthcare professionals from lower-income countries to higher-income countries. This movement depletes the healthcare workforce in the origin countries, weakening their health systems and worsening health outcomes.Intervening Obstacle: A barrier that hinders or stops a migrant from reaching their intended destination.Example: A desert, a mountain range, strict visa requirements, or a lack of funds.Intervening Opportunity: An alternative attractive destination that arises between a migrant's origin and their intended final destination, causing them to settle there instead.Example: A migrant aiming for Europe finds a good job and a safe community in a transit country like Turkey, deciding to stay there.2.3ESPN","FactorCategoryDescriptionPush,Factors,Reasons,Leave,Pull,Factors,Reasons,Come,E,EconomicMoney,jobs,financialopportunities,standard,living,High,unemployment,low,wages,poverty,lack,ofeconomic,opportunity,high,cost,living,Higher,wages,better,job,prospects,economic,growth,lower,cost,ofliving,educational,trainingopportunities,SocialCulture,family,community,discrimination,social,services,quality,life,Discrimination,persecution,religious,ethnic,lackof,social,services,healthcare,education,familyseparation,limited,social,freedoms,Presence,family,friends,chainmigration,cultural,affinity,betteraccess,social,services,greatersocial,freedoms,acceptance,betterquality,life,P,PoliticalGovernment,stability,conflict,human,rights,safety,War,political,instability,persecution,government,lack,human,rights,corruption,forceddisplacement,lack,safety,security,Political,stability,safety,security,protection,human,rights,goodgovernance,asylum,opportunities,freedom,conflict,N,EnvironmentalNatural,world,climate,resources,disasters,Natural,disasters,floods,droughts,earthquakes,climate,change,impacts,sea,level,rise,desertification,resource,scarcity,water,arableland,pollution,environmental,degradation,makingan,area,uninhabitable,Safer,environment,fertile,land,reliable,water,sources,less,exposureto,natural,hazards,cleanerenvironment,Medical,brain,drain,emigration,trained,healthcare,professionals,lower,income,countries,higher,income,countries,movement,depletes,healthcare,workforce,origin,countries,weakening,health,systems,worsening,health,outcomes,Intervening,Obstacle,barrier,hinders,stops,migrant,reaching,intended,destination,Example,desert,mountain,range,strict,visa,requirements,lack,funds,Intervening,Opportunity,alternative,attractive,destination,arises,migrant,orig