AP Precalculus Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"10.4 Solving trigonometric equations - Equivalent trigonometric forms may be useful in solving trigonometric equations. 10.4.A.3 Because trigonometric functions are periodic, there are often infinitely many solutions to trigonometric equations. 10.4.A.4 Algebraic and graphical methods are used to solve trigonometric equations in a finite interval, including the use of trigonometric identities and factorization. - Inverse trigonometric functions are useful in solving equations involving trigonometric functions, but solutions may need to be modified due to domain restrictions. 10.4.A.6 In trigonometric equations arising from a contextual scenario, there is often a domain restriction that can be implied from the context, which limits the number of solutions. - Equivalent trigonometric forms may be useful in solving trigonometric inequalities. 10.4.B.2 Inverse trigonometric functions are useful in solving inequalities involving trigonometric functions, but solutions may need to be modified due to domain restrictions. 10.4.B.3 In trigonometric inequalities arising from a contextual scenario, there is often a domain restriction that can be implied from the context, which limits the number of solutions. 11.1 Polar coordinates and graphs - Polar coordinates The polar coordinate system is an alternative to the Cartesian system of rectangular coordinates for locating points in a plane. λ The polar coordinates of a point P in the polar coordinate system consist of an","Solving,trigonometric,equations,Equivalent,trigonometric,forms,may,useful,solving,trigonometric,equations,trigonometric,functions,periodic,often,infinitely,many,solutions,trigonometric,equations,Algebraic,graphical,methods,used,solve,trigonometric,equations,finite,interval,including,use,trigonometric,identities,factorization,Inverse,trigonometric,functions,useful,solving,equations,involving,trigonometric,functions,solutions,may,need,modified,due,domain,restrictions,trigonometric,equations,arising,contextual,scenario,often,domain,restriction,implied,context,limits,number,solutions,Equivalent,trigonometric,forms,may,useful,solving,trigonometric,inequalities,B,Inverse,trigonometric,functions,useful,solving,inequalities,involving,trigonometric,functions,solutions,may,need,modified,due,domain,restrictions,B,trigonometric,inequalities,arising,contextual,scenario,often,domain,restriction,implied,context,limits,number,solutions,Polar,coordinates,graphs,Polar,coordinates,polar,coordinate,system,alternative,Cartesian,system,rectangular,coordinates,locating,points,plane,polar,coordinates,point,P,polar,coordinate,system,consist"
AP Precalculus Review Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"ordered pair (r,θ). The directed distance from the pole to P is r, and the measure of the angle from the polar axis to OP is θ . The polar coordinates of the pole O are (0,θ ) . Angle θ can be measured in degrees or radians. Both r and θ can be either positive or negative. When θ is positive, the polar angle is obtained by rotating OP counterclockwise from the polar axis, and when θis negative, the polar angle is obtained by rotating OP clockwise from the polar axis. When r is positive, the polar distance is measured from O along the terminal side of the angle θ , and When r is negative, the polar distance is measured from O on the ray opposite the terminal side of the angle θ. A plane is called an rθ -plane when polar coordinates (r,θ ) are used to identify its points.","ordered,pair,r,directed,distance,pole,P,r,measure,angle,polar,axis,OP,polar,coordinates,pole,Angle,measured,degrees,radians,r,either,positive,negative,positive,polar,angle,obtained,rotating,OP,counterclockwise,polar,axis,negative,polar,angle,obtained,rotating,OP,clockwise,polar,axis,r,positive,polar,distance,measured,along,terminal,side,angle,r,negative,polar,distance,measured,ray,opposite,terminal,side,angle,plane,called,r,plane,polar,coordinates,r,used,identify,points"
