Reivew Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Part I: Historical Periods of Music   Middle Ages (ca. 500–1400) •Key word: Dark •Context: Dominated by the Church; music served religious rituals. •Style: oMonophony: Single melodic line (e.g., Gregorian chant). oNeumes: Early notation (notation for Gregorian Chant) •Forms/Genres: oPlainchant (aka Gregorian chants): Sacred songs in Latin. •Key Composer: Hildegard of Bingen — mystic nun, prolific composer. •Listening Focus:  oChant-like quality omodal scales (Ionian, Dorian, Phrygian, Lydian, Mixolydian, Aeolian, Locrian) olack of steady beat   Renaissance (ca. 1400–1600) •Key word: humanism •Context: rise of secularism, invention of the printing press. •Style: oPolyphony: Interweaving independent lines. oWord pain;ng: Music literally illustrates lyrics (e.g., “rise rise rise rise”, with each “rise” one semitone higher). •Genres: oMass (sacred): polyphonic (many voice simulatenously) or plainchant oMotet (sacred): choral composition, standalone piece, polyphonic, freer than Mass and not always for worship oMadrigal (secular): V ocal secular music •Key Composers: Josquin des Prez (wrote motets), Palestrina (wrote mass) •Listening Focus:","Part,Historical,Periods,Music,Middle,Ages,ca,Key,word,Dark,Context,Dominated,Church,music,served,religious,rituals,Style,oMonophony,Single,melodic,line,e,g,Gregorian,chant,oNeumes,Early,notation,notation,Gregorian,Chant,Forms,Genres,oPlainchant,aka,Gregorian,chants,Sacred,songs,Latin,Key,Composer,Hildegard,Bingen,mystic,nun,prolific,composer,Listening,Focus,oChant,like,quality,omodal,scales,Ionian,Dorian,Phrygian,Lydian,Mixolydian,Aeolian,Locrian,olack,steady,beat,Renaissance,ca,Key,word,humanism,Context,rise,secularism,invention,printing,press,Style,oPolyphony,Interweaving,independent,lines,oWord,pain,ng,Music,literally,illustrates,lyrics,e,g,rise,rise,rise,rise,rise,one,semitone,higher,Genres,oMass,sacred,polyphonic,many,voice,simulatenously,plainchant,oMotet,sacred,choral,composition,standalone,piece,polyphonic,freer,Mass,always,worship,oMadrigal,secular,V,ocal,secular,music,Key,Composers,Josquin,des,Prez,wrote,motets,Palestrina,wrote,mass,Listening,Focus"
Reivew Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"oClarity of text oImitative textures: polyphonic texture where one voice or part imitates the melody of another voice or part, usually at a slightly later time  oModal harmony: no structural harmony (like V-I), but still has tonal center (Mode)   Baroque (1600–1750) •Key word: absolu>sm •Context: dramatic arts (drastic contrast in color; characters in bloody motion), Versailles = music center, Counter-Reformation •Style: oUnity of mood: maintaining constant emotional atmosphere oRhythmic drive: strong sense of momentum, maintained usually by drums/bass oBasso con;nuo: continuous bassline played by one instrument (can change, vary) oTerraced dynamics: Abrupt shifts between loud/soft dynamics. •Key Forms/Genres: oFugue: Musical structure, counterpoint with subject/answer/exposition/development/coda oConcerto Grosso: Dialogue between soloists (concertino) and orchestra (ripieno). oOpera: dramatic work/story with mostly singing and orchestral accompaniment oOratorio: large-scale musical composition for orchestra, choir, and soloists oCantata: vocal composition with instrumental accompaniment, sacred/secular oToccata: free-flowing, improvisational, organ/harpsichord •Key Composers: J.S. Bach (famous for fugues), G.F. Handel (“Hallelujah” from Messiah), Vivaldi (four seasons), Monteverdi, Pachelbel (canon in d) •Listening Focus:  oHarpsichord: piano, except strings are plucked oOrnamentation: additional, decorative elements (e.g. trills) oContinuous melody: Uninterrupted flow oContrapuntal complexity: elaborate uses of counterpoints (two or more melodic lines at the same time)","oClarity,text,oImitative,textures,polyphonic,texture,one,voice,part,imitates,melody,another,voice,part,usually,slightly,later,time,oModal,harmony,structural,harmony,like,V,still,tonal,center,Mode,Baroque,Key,word,absolu,sm,Context,dramatic,arts,drastic,contrast,color,characters,bloody,motion,Versailles,music,center,Counter,Reformation,Style,oUnity,mood,maintaining,constant,emotional,atmosphere,oRhythmic,drive,strong,sense,momentum,maintained,usually,drums,bass,oBasso,con,nuo,continuous,bassline,played,one,instrument,change,vary,oTerraced,dynamics,Abrupt,shifts,loud,soft,dynamics,Key,Forms,Genres,oFugue,Musical,structure,counterpoint,subject,answer,exposition,development,coda,oConcerto,Grosso,Dialogue,soloists,concertino,orchestra,ripieno,oOpera,dramatic,work,story,mostly,singing,orchestral,accompaniment,oOratorio,large,scale,musical,composition,orchestra,choir,soloists,oCantata,vocal,composition,instrumental,accompaniment,sacred,secular,oToccata,free,flowing,improvisational,organ,harpsichord,Key,Composers,J,Bach,famous,fugues,G,F,Handel,Hallelujah,Messiah,Vivaldi,four,seasons,Monteverdi,Pachelbel,canon,Listening,Focus,oHarpsichord,piano,except,strings,plucked,oOrnamentation,additional,decorative,elements,e,g,trills,oContinuous,melody,Uninterrupted,flow,oContrapuntal,complexity,elaborate,uses,counterpoints,two,melodic,lines,time"
Reivew Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"Classical (1750–1820) •Key word: enlightenment •Context: Enlightenment ideals—balance, clarity, form. •Style: oHomophony: Melody with accompaniment (harmony).  oBalanced phrases: musical structure with two or more phrases (musical sentences that convey ideas) of equal length and similar tension oSimple harmony: Two or more notes played together pleasingly, e.g. triads oClear cadences: Cadence with clear conclusion (Authentic V-I, Plagal IV-I) •Key Forms/Genres: oSonata form: Exposition – Development – Recapitulation. oSymphony: a large-scale musical composition for orchestra, fast-slow-dance-fast oConcerto: musical composition for one solo instrument oString Quartet: Two violins, a viola, a cello oZu 2: 2 instruments play in unision •Key Composers: Haydn (father of string quartet and symphony), Mozart (prodigy, famous for his nine symphonies), early Beethoven    RomanGc (1820–1900) •Key word: individualism •Context: nationalism, Industrial Revolution. •Themes: oLove, death, nature, the supernatural, exoticism. •Musical Features: oProgram Music: Tells a story (e.g., Symphonie Fantas;que). oRubato: speeds up/slows down time, overall duration intact oExpanded orchestration: increasing the orchestra size and types instruments oVirtuosity: extreme technical skill (e.g. paganini and liszt) oSelf-expression: composers tried to express their inner feelings through the pieces of music they create •Key Composers:","Classical,Key,word,enlightenment,Context,Enlightenment,ideals,balance,clarity,form,Style,oHomophony,Melody,accompaniment,harmony,oBalanced,phrases,musical,structure,two,phrases,musical,sentences,convey,ideas,equal,length,similar,tension,oSimple,harmony,Two,notes,played,together,pleasingly,e,g,triads,oClear,cadences,Cadence,clear,conclusion,Authentic,V,Plagal,IV,Key,Forms,Genres,oSonata,form,Exposition,Development,Recapitulation,oSymphony,large,scale,musical,composition,orchestra,fast,slow,dance,fast,oConcerto,musical,composition,one,solo,instrument,oString,Quartet,Two,violins,viola,cello,oZu,instruments,play,unision,Key,Composers,Haydn,father,string,quartet,symphony,Mozart,prodigy,famous,nine,symphonies,early,Beethoven,RomanGc,Key,word,individualism,Context,nationalism,Industrial,Revolution,Themes,oLove,death,nature,supernatural,exoticism,Musical,Features,oProgram,Music,Tells,story,e,g,Symphonie,Fantas,que,oRubato,speeds,slows,time,overall,duration,intact,oExpanded,orchestration,increasing,orchestra,size,types,instruments,oVirtuosity,extreme,technical,skill,e,g,paganini,liszt,oSelf,expression,composers,tried,express,inner,feelings,pieces,music,create,Key,Composers"
Reivew Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"oBeethoven (late): lost hearing, much more romantic (e.g. he expressed his frustration through his music) oChopin: Polish, weak, wrote nocturnes (quiet, calm, beautiful pieces), “poet of the piano” oLiszt: Pianist with big hands, handsome womaniser, plays difficult pieces for fun oBerlioz: Unconventional music, fell in love and wrote Symphonie Fantastique (dude kills his love and goes to hell and meets her) oBrahms: Conservative, german, friends ;) with Clara Schumann oTchaikovsky: Russian ballet master (nutcracker, swan lake), gay oMendelssohn: Bach glazer, got advice from sister Fanny, died at 38 from overwork (bro is an SHSID student) oSchubert: hard-working Beethoven glazer wrote Lied(er) (Art song, setting of poem to music, e.g. Erlking) oRichard Wagner: Hungarian, Jew-hater, Hitler’s a fan, had Leitmotif (musical theme that suggests a person, idea, or event) oPuccini: Italian, Verismo (operas that portrayed real people), La Boheme (real, difficult life of musicians, philosophers, playwrights…), soaring melodies •Listening Focus: Rich harmonies, dynamic contrasts, expressive melodies, experimenting with dissonance  Modern Era & the “Isms” (1900–Present) Context: Two world wars, technology, global influence, philosophical shifts.  Impressionism •Inspira>on: Claude Monet’s painting Impression: Sunrise. •Musical Traits: oWhole-tone/pentatonic/modal scales. oColor and texture over clear form. oExtended chords (9ths, 13ths), floating rhythms. •Key Composers: Claude Debussy (Clair de Lune), Maurice Ravel. PrimiCvism •Inspira>on: Folk art, tribal rhythms, prehistoric themes. •Traits: oSavage rhythms","oBeethoven,late,lost,hearing,much,romantic,e,g,expressed,frustration,music,oChopin,Polish,weak,wrote,nocturnes,quiet,calm,beautiful,pieces,poet,piano,oLiszt,Pianist,big,hands,handsome,womaniser,plays,difficult,pieces,fun,oBerlioz,Unconventional,music,fell,love,wrote,Symphonie,Fantastique,dude,kills,love,goes,hell,meets,oBrahms,Conservative,german,friends,Clara,Schumann,oTchaikovsky,Russian,ballet,master,nutcracker,swan,lake,gay,oMendelssohn,Bach,glazer,got,advice,sister,Fanny,died,overwork,bro,SHSID,student,oSchubert,hard,working,Beethoven,glazer,wrote,Lied,er,Art,song,setting,poem,music,e,g,Erlking,oRichard,Wagner,Hungarian,Jew,hater,Hitler,fan,Leitmotif,musical,theme,suggests,person,idea,event,oPuccini,Italian,Verismo,operas,portrayed,real,people,La,Boheme,real,difficult,life,musicians,philosophers,playwrights,soaring,melodies,Listening,Focus,Rich,harmonies,dynamic,contrasts,expressive,melodies,experimenting,dissonance,Modern,Era,Isms,Present,Context,Two,world,wars,technology,global,influence,philosophical,shifts,Impressionism,Inspira,Claude,Monet,painting,Impression,Sunrise,Musical,Traits,oWhole,tone,pentatonic,modal,scales,oColor,texture,clear,form,oExtended,chords,ths,ths,floating,rhythms,Key,Composers,Claude,Debussy,Clair,de,Lune,Maurice,Ravel,PrimiCvism,Inspira,Folk,art,tribal,rhythms,prehistoric,themes,Traits,oSavage,rhythms"
Reivew Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"oOstinatos: short, repetitive musical phrases, typically in the same pitch or rhythm oPolytonality: two or more keys/tonal centers used simultaneously oHarsh dissonance: exactly what it sounds like oSimple motives: Short, recognizable ideas (e.g. Mission Impossible theme) •Key Work: Stravinsky’s The Rite of Spring (Yinte likes this a lot)  Expressionism •Themes: Inner anxiety, nightmares, alienation. •Traits: oAtonality, extreme dissonance, Sprechstimme (spoken singing). •Key Composers: Schoenberg, Berg, Webern. •Techniques: oTwelve-tone system: Serialism using tone rows. Minimalism •Traits: Repetition, gradual change, steady pulse. •Composers: Steve Reich (Clapping Music), Philip Glass.  Electronic & Chance (Aleatoric, indeterminate) Music •Traits: Synthesizers, tape loops, ambient textures, chance (luck and randomness part of composition) •Figures:  oJohn Cage (4’33”): music professor, what is music? oVarese (Poème électronique):  Poème électronique = electronic music pioneer oKraftwerk: pioneered, electric-synth, techno, and electronic dance music Part II: Instruments & OrchestraInstrument Families •Strings: Violin, viola, cello (violoncello), double bass, harp. oPlayed with bow (arco) or plucked (pizzicato). •Woodwinds: Flute (flauto), oboe (oboi), clarinet (Bb), bassoon (Faggotti), countrabassoon  oTone produced via vibrating air column.","oOstinatos,short,repetitive,musical,phrases,typically,pitch,rhythm,oPolytonality,two,keys,tonal,centers,used,simultaneously,oHarsh,dissonance,exactly,sounds,like,oSimple,motives,Short,recognizable,ideas,e,g,Mission,Impossible,theme,Key,Work,Stravinsky,Rite,Spring,Yinte,likes,lot,Expressionism,Themes,Inner,anxiety,nightmares,alienation,Traits,oAtonality,extreme,dissonance,Sprechstimme,spoken,singing,Key,Composers,Schoenberg,Berg,Webern,Techniques,oTwelve,tone,system,Serialism,using,tone,rows,Minimalism,Traits,Repetition,gradual,change,steady,pulse,Composers,Steve,Reich,Clapping,Music,Philip,Glass,Electronic,Chance,Aleatoric,indeterminate,Music,Traits,Synthesizers,tape,loops,ambient,textures,chance,luck,randomness,part,composition,Figures,oJohn,Cage,music,professor,music,oVarese,Po,lectronique,Po,lectronique,electronic,music,pioneer,oKraftwerk,pioneered,electric,synth,techno,electronic,dance,music,Part,II,Instruments,OrchestraInstrument,Families,Strings,Violin,viola,cello,violoncello,double,bass,harp,oPlayed,bow,arco,plucked,pizzicato,Woodwinds,Flute,flauto,oboe,oboi,clarinet,Bb,bassoon,Faggotti,countrabassoon,oTone,produced,via,vibrating,air,column"
Reivew Guide.pdf,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"•Brass: Trumpet (Bb), French horn (F, written as C, Corni), trombone (trombe), tuba (C/Bb). oLips buzz into a cup-shaped mouthpiece. •Percussion: oPitched: Timpani, xylophone. oUnpitched: Snare drum, cymbals. •Keyboards: Piano (strings struck), organ, harpsichord. •Electronic: Synthesizers, samplers.    The Orchestra Structure & SeaCng: •Strings front, woodwinds center, brass back, percussion rear. •Modern orchestras often include over 90 musicians. EvoluCon Highlights: •Baroque: Small, harpsichord-centered ensembles. •Classical: Balanced, with winds used in pairs, less bass •Roman>c: Larger, with added instruments (tuba, cymbals, harp) and expanded size of existing parts of the orchestra •Modern: Expanded percussion, exotic instruments, electronics. Examples: •Haydn’s Symphony No. 31 (Classical) •Wagner’s Ride of the Valkyries (Romantic) •Stravinsky’s The Rite of Spring (Modern) Part III: Transposing1.Understand the key of the instrument  a.Bb: Clarinet, trumpet, cornet (when they see a C on the score, it’s actually a Bb when they blow it out) b.F: French horn, English horn (P5 lower), Alto Clarinet c.Eb: Alto (M6 lower) + Baritone Sax (M6+P8 lower), Eb Clarinet (m3 higher) d.G: Alto ﬂute (P4 lower)","Brass,Trumpet,Bb,French,horn,F,written,C,Corni,trombone,trombe,tuba,C,Bb,oLips,buzz,cup,shaped,mouthpiece,Percussion,oPitched,Timpani,xylophone,oUnpitched,Snare,drum,cymbals,Keyboards,Piano,strings,struck,organ,harpsichord,Electronic,Synthesizers,samplers,Orchestra,Structure,SeaCng,Strings,front,woodwinds,center,brass,back,percussion,rear,Modern,orchestras,often,include,musicians,EvoluCon,Highlights,Baroque,Small,harpsichord,centered,ensembles,Classical,Balanced,winds,used,pairs,less,bass,Roman,c,Larger,added,instruments,tuba,cymbals,harp,expanded,size,existing,parts,orchestra,Modern,Expanded,percussion,exotic,instruments,electronics,Examples,Haydn,Symphony,Classical,Wagner,Ride,Valkyries,Romantic,Stravinsky,Rite,Spring,Modern,Par