7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Singular subject with singular verb: When the subject of a sentence is singular, the verb must also be in the singular form.","Singular,subject,singular,verb,subject,sentence,singular,verb,must,also,singular,form"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Plural subject with plural verb: If the subject is plural, the verb takes the plural form.","Plural,subject,plural,verb,subject,plural,verb,takes,plural,form"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,2) Indefinite Pronouns,"Indefinite,Pronouns"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Some indefinite pronouns are always singular: Words like “everyone,” “everybody,” “someone,” “somebody,” “no one,” “nobody,” “anyone,” “anybody,” “each,” and “either” take a singular verb.","indefinite,pronouns,always,singular,Words,like,everyone,everybody,someone,somebody,one,nobody,anyone,anybody,either,take,singular,verb"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Some indefinite pronouns are plural: “Both,” “few,” “many,” and “several” are plural and take a plural verb.","indefinite,pronouns,plural,many,several,plural,take,plural,verb"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Some can be singular or plural depending on context: “All,” “some,” “none,” “any”. When referring to a countable noun, they can be plural; when referring to an uncountable noun, they are singular.","singular,plural,depending,context,none,referring,countable,noun,plural,referring,uncountable,noun,singular"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,3) Compound Subjects,"Compound,Subjects"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Joined by “and”: Usually, compound subjects joined by “and” are plural and take a plural verb.\-Joined by “or”/“nor”: The verb agrees with the subject closest to it.","Joined,Usually,compound,subjects,joined,plural,take,plural,verb,Joined,verb,agrees,subject,closest"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,Collective Nouns,"Collective,Nouns"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Can be singular or plural: Collective nouns like “family,” “team,” “class,” “committee” can be treated as singular when the group is acting as a single unit. For example, “The family is going on vacation.” But they can be plural when the members of the group are acting individually.","singular,plural,Collective,nouns,like,family,team,class,committee,treated,singular,group,acting,single,unit,example,family,going,vacation,plural,members,group,acting,individually"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,5) Subject - Verb Agreement with Phrases,"Subject,Verb,Agreement,Phrases"
7 IDX G9 ENG S+.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,-Prepositional phrases do not affect subject - verb agreement: The subject is not part of the prepositional phrase.,"Prepositional,phrases,affect,subject,verb,agreement,subject,part,prepositional,phrase"
