1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Inductive Reasoning: A type of logical thinking that involves forming 	generalizations based on specific incidents you've experienced, observations 	you've made, or facts you know to be true or false.","Inductive,Reasoning,type,logical,thinking,involves,forming,generalizations,based,specific,incidents,experienced,observations,made,facts,know,true,false"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Deductive Reasoning: A type of logical thinking where you start with a general 	fact, rule, or premise and then deduce or infer certain specific details or 	conclusions.","Deductive,Reasoning,type,logical,thinking,start,general,fact,rule,premise,deduce,infer,certain,specific,details,conclusions"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,Logic Statements,"Logic,Statements"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Proposition: Also known as a statement, a proposition is a declarative sentence that 	is either true or false but not both.","Proposition,Also,known,statement,proposition,declarative,sentence,either,true,false"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Compound Proposition: This is a combination of two or more propositions using 	logical operators. They include conjunctions, disjunctions, implications, and 	biconditionals.","Compound,Proposition,combination,two,propositions,using,logical,operators,include,conjunctions,disjunctions,implications,biconditionals"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Conjunction: A conjunction is a compound proposition that uses the logical 	operator AND. For the conjunction p ∧ q to be true, both p and q must be true.","Conjunction,conjunction,compound,proposition,uses,logical,operator,conjunction,p,q,true,p,q,must,true"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Disjunction: A disjunction is a compound proposition that uses the logical operator 	OR. For the disjunction p ∨ q, it is enough for either p or q to be true for the entire 	disjunction to be true.","Disjunction,disjunction,compound,proposition,uses,logical,operator,disjunction,p,q,enough,either,p,q,true,entire,disjunction,true"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Negation: The negation of a proposition p is ""not p"", denoted as ~p or ¬p. If p is 	true, then ~p is false, and vice versa.","Negation,negation,proposition,p,p,denoted,p,p,p,true,p,false,vice,versa"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Conditional Statement (Implication): This is a compound proposition of the form ""if 	p then q"" represented as p → q. It is false only when p is true and q is false; 	otherwise, it is true.","Conditional,Statement,Implication,compound,proposition,form,p,q,represented,p,q,false,p,true,q,false,otherwise,true"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Biconditional Statement: A biconditional statement of the form ""p if and only if q"" is 	true when p and q have the same truth values, and false when they are different. It is 	represented as p ↔ q or p ≡ q.","Biconditional,Statement,biconditional,statement,form,p,q,true,p,q,truth,values,false,different,represented,p,q,p,q"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,-Contradiction: A compound proposition that is always false regardless of the truth 	values of the individual propositions that constitute it.,"Contradiction,compound,proposition,always,false,regardless,truth,values,individual,propositions,constitute"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,-Contingency: A compound proposition that could be either true or false depending 	on the truth values of its constituent propositions.,"Contingency,compound,proposition,could,either,true,false,depending,truth,values,constituent,propositions"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,-Satisfiability: A proposition is satisfiable if there is at least one assignment of truth values to its variables that makes it true.,"Satisfiability,proposition,satisfiable,least,one,assignment,truth,values,variables,makes,true"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Sufficient: When given A, we can know B then A is sufficient for B","Sufficient,given,know,B,sufficient,B"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Neccesary: When given A, we can know B then B is neccesary for A.","Neccesary,given,know,B,B,neccesary"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,Operations:,Operations
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"In a conditional statement of the form ""if p then q"", also written as ""p → q"", we have different forms created by switching or negating the hypothesis and conclusion:","conditional,statement,form,p,q,also,written,p,q,different,forms,created,switching,negating,hypothesis,conclusion"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Converse: The converse of ""if p then q"" is ""if q then p"", or q → p. This is formed by 	switching the hypothesis and the conclusion.","Converse,converse,p,q,q,p,q,p,formed,switching,hypothesis,conclusion"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Inverse: The inverse of ""if p then q"" is ""if not p then not q"", or ¬p → ¬q. This is 		formed by negating both the hypothesis and conclusion.","Inverse,inverse,p,q,p,q,p,q,formed,negating,hypothesis,conclusion"
1 G9 MATH H.docx,2025-06-27 02:49:14,"-Contrapositive: The contrapositive of ""if p then q"" is ""if not q then not p"", or ¬q → 	¬p. This is formed by both switching and negating the hypothesis and conclusion.","Contrapositive,contrapositive,p,q,q,p,q,p,formed,switching,negating,hypothesis,conclusion"
